  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gradual warmup brings high temperatures from the 50s \nto the 60s and 70s Thursday and Friday as the prolonged dry stretch \ncontinues. Windy conditions arrive Friday ahead of a weak cold \nfront. \n\nCold start to the day today as efficient radiational cooling has \nbrought some of the coldest air of the season to the region. Lows \nhave ranged from the lower 40s across urban areas to the lower 20s \nin the valleys of northern Massachusetts. Should see temperatures \nrebound fairly quickly by the early morning under full sun. Still, \nit will be somewhat cool today with high temperatures in the mid 50s \nin the interior and near 60 across the lower CT River Valley. Warmer \nlows Wednesday night as mid and high clouds overspread the region \nduring the first half of the night. A more narrow window of clearing \nthrough the second half of the night will likely result in lows \nrunning about 5-10 degrees warmer. \n\nExpecting a rebound in higher temperatures Thursday as a surge of \nwarm advection ahead of a cold front warms 925 mb temperatures to \nbetween +11 and +15C.This will yield high temperatures in the mid-\n60s on Thursday with a few isolated readings near 70 in the \nConnecticut and Merrimack River Valleys. Despite the mid-level \nzonal flow Friday (ahead of our next weak wave), the westerly flow \nwill support efficient mixing in the boundary layer. This will \nresult in a gusty/windier day and likely the warmest day of the week \nwith high temperatures in the upper 60s to low 70s. Some of the \nstrongest gusts will likely be found downwind of the Berkshires with \na favorable wind direction for downsloping and thus locally \nhigher gusts. NBM continues to highlight probabilities 50-75%+ \nfor temperatures 70F or greater with the highest probabilities \nfor the valleys and I-95 metros. This is reasonable given the \nadded factor of the warming downslope flow. A mild late-October \nday is looking likely. \n\nAnother moisture-starved shortwave and cold front moves overnight \nFriday. This will shift temperatures back into the lower 60s this \nweekend, which is seasonable for late October.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Transition to a more unsettled weather pattern by\nearly next week bringing periodic chances for rain. Confidence \nin specific timing remains low. \n\nLooking ahead to next week, ensemble continues to show a rather \nchaotic upper level pattern unfolding across the CONUS. A deep \ntrough digs over the western US as a series of embedded shortwaves \neject into the plains and track E. Guidance shows a wide range of \nsolutions with the eventual tracks of these systems as they approach \nthe region early next week. Many factors, namely the extent and \nmagnitude of upper level confluence will govern the strength and \ntrack of any eventual storms. At the moment, think the NBM has a \ngood handle on POPs Sunday night into Tuesday with probabilities \nincreasing to between 20 and 45 percent.",
